 ## Overview
-This method enables real-time visualization in surgical simulators by combining volume-rendered 3D medical images (organs) with surface-rendered medical instruments (e.g., needles) using shear-warp factorization [S1, S2]. It solves the performance bottleneck of rendering complex, fuzzy volumetric data alongside fast-moving, simple geometric instruments by leveraging early ray termination and instrument-region masking in the sheared image space [S1, S2]. Unlike pure volume rendering, which requires traversing the entire dataset for every view change, or pure surface rendering, which requires difficult and noise-sensitive segmentation of medical organs, this hybrid approach assigns surface rendering to instruments for motion efficiency and volume rendering to organs for structural fidelity [S1, S2].
+This method enables real-time visualization in surgical simulators by combining volume-rendered 3D medical images (organs) with surface-rendered medical instruments (e.g., needles) using shear-warp factorization [S1, S2]. It solves the performance bottleneck of rendering dynamic instruments against static volumetric data by leveraging the speed of surface rendering for instruments and the segmentation-free realism of volume rendering for organs [S1, S2]. Unlike standard shear-warp which renders only volume data, this approach integrates a Z-buffer comparison in the intermediate sheared space to correctly handle depth occlusion between the two modalities, using early ray termination and instrument-region masking to reduce computation [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The method builds on the [Shear-Warp Algorithm](4304-shear-warp-algorithm.md), which decomposes the viewing transformation into a 3D shear, a projection, and a 2D warp to facilitate efficient interpolation [S2]. The core innovation is the integration of surface-rendered instrument depth cues into the volume rendering pipeline to optimize computation and correctly composite overlapping elements.
+The method extends the standard shear-warp algorithm [24-shear-warp.md] by introducing a hybrid rendering pipeline that processes surface and volume data in a shared intermediate coordinate system.
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
 **Inputs:**
-*   3D volume data (e.g., CT/MRI scans of organs) with associated transfer functions for color and opacity [S1, S2].
-*   3D geometric model of the medical instrument (surface primitives) [S1, S2].
-*   View matrix $V$ defining the viewer's position and orientation [S1, S2].
-*   Instrument state (position, rotation, translation) [S1, S2].
+*   3D volume dataset (e.g., CT/MRI) representing organs.
+*   3D surface model of the medical instrument (polygon mesh).
+*   View matrix $M_{view}$ defining the camera position and orientation.
+*   Instrument motion parameters (rotation/translation) if dynamic.
 
 **Outputs:**
-*   A 2D combined image $C(u, v)$ displayed on the screen [S1, S2].
+*   A combined 2D image $C(u, v)$ in the final screen space.
 
